From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 00:33:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: a widget teardown does not take the desktop with it

The first run on a tty with a connected panel modeset a 2880x1920@120
connector, logged `the DRM thread confirmed the modeset`, and SEGV'd in the
browser process about 200ms later with nothing ever drawn:

    Received signal 11 SEGV_MAPERR 000000000088
    #4 views::DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform::CloseNow()
    #5 views::DesktopNativeWidgetAura::~DesktopNativeWidgetAura()
    #7 views::Widget::~Widget()
    #8 (anonymous namespace)::StatusIconWidget::~StatusIconWidget()
    #9 base::TaskAnnotator::RunTaskImpl()

`0x88` is `compositor()` being null: `WindowTreeHost::compositor()` is
`compositor_.get()`, and `CloseNow()` calls
`SetExternalBeginFrameControllerClientFactory` on it one line past a guard that
null-checks `platform_window()` and not the compositor.

FRAME 8 IS NOT THE STATUS ICON, and reading it as one would have fixed the
wrong thing. `StatusIconButtonLinux::OnSetDelegate` refuses to build its widget
at all unless `GetPlatformRuntimeProperties().supports_system_tray_windowing`
is true; that field defaults to false, only `ozone_platform_x11.cc` sets it,
and `OzonePlatformDrm::GetPlatformRuntimeProperties` returns a
`host_properties_` that never touches it -- a grep of the whole tree finds
three references and none of them is ozone/drm. So on this platform
`StatusIconWidget` is unconstructable and the guard did fire.

The name is an ICF fold. `use_icf` is on for the shipped engine (`is_posix &&
!is_debug`, and it is neither a sanitizer nor a coverage build), and the
deleting destructor of a `views::Widget` subclass that adds no members is
`call views::Widget::~Widget; jmp operator delete` -- identical instructions
AND identical relocations to every other such subclass's, and to
`views::Widget`'s own, because the operand of `operator delete` is the same
`sizeof`. Compiled at this pin's clang, two such destructors come out
byte-for-byte equal with no vptr store between them. `symbol_level = 0` leaves
`.symtab` in place, so `base/third_party/symbolize` names the folded address
from whichever symbol it meets; on a Linux build `StatusIconWidget` is the only
anonymous-namespace `views::Widget` subclass there is to meet. What frame 8
actually establishes is "a `views::Widget` was deleted from a posted task", and
nothing narrower.

WHAT THE HALF-BUILT HOST REALLY IS. `compositor_` is only ever cleared in two
places: `~WindowTreeHostPlatform`, where the object is dying, and
`DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform::CloseNow`. So frame 4 is the SECOND `CloseNow`
on that host, and the first one left the wreckage. `CloseNow` ends
`DestroyCompositor(); platform_window()->Close();` -- and
`DrmWindowHost::Close()` was `{}`, alone among Ozone platforms.
`PlatformWindow::Close()` is a request whose completion is
`PlatformWindowDelegate::OnClosed()`: `HeadlessWindow::Close()` is nothing
else, `WaylandWindow::Close()` is nothing else, `X11Window::Close()` tears the
X window down and then makes it. `OnClosed()` is what runs
`SetPlatformWindow(nullptr)` and `OnHostClosed()`, which is what destroys the
host and the `DesktopNativeWidgetAura` and clears `Widget::native_widget_`.
None of that happened, so the widget's own destruction later found a live
native widget over a live platform window over a destroyed compositor.
Upstream states the invariant one line into `~DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform`:

    DCHECK(!platform_window()) << "The host must be closed before destroying it.";

`dcheck_always_on = false` in the shipped build, so the assertion that would
have named this is compiled out and what is left is the SEGV.

WHY HERE AND NOT THE THREE OTHER PLACES IT COULD GO.

Not "stop the widget being created": the widget is not the status icon, the
guard that would have refused one is already firing, and every widget a views
browser makes hits this. A fix at the widget removes one crash and leaves the
platform unable to close any window.

Not "make the software-compositing refusal stop producing a half-built host":
the refusal has nothing to do with it. `force_software_compositing` does not
skip the compositor -- `DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform::Init` calls
`CreateCompositor(params.force_software_compositing || type == TYPE_TOOLTIP,
...)` unconditionally, and `WindowTreeHost::CreateCompositor` always
`make_unique`s one. ozone/drm's refusal is one `LOG(ERROR)` in
`GbmSurfaceFactory::CreateCanvasForWidget`, in the GPU process, at the display
compositor; it cannot reach `WindowTreeHost::compositor_`.

Not "null-check `compositor()` in `CloseNow()`": that would make the second
`CloseNow` survive a state that must not exist. The host would still never be
destroyed, the `DrmWindowHost` would stay registered in `DrmWindowHostManager`,
`GpuDestroyWindow` would never be sent, and the window would go on owning a
CRTC nobody can close. The crash is the only report there is that the close
never happened, and quieting it would cost the report.

So: the same shape as `0015`'s seven `NOTREACHED()`s and `0018`'s empty
`SetFullscreen` -- a stub whose ChromeOS premise is that ash never routes a
window through `DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform`, reached by a views browser on a
tty, where the premise is false. `delegate_->OnClosed()` is the last statement,
because it destroys this object from inside the call.

NO UNIT TEST, and that is not an omission dressed up. The fix is one call with
no decision in it: there is no branch, no state and no injectable I/O, and
`DrmWindowHost` takes six collaborators before it can be constructed. A gtest
here would assert that a line of code is the line of code it is. What can be
checked cheaply is that the line is there at all, which is the same species of
one-line-that-must-exist as the missing `GetElementType` override, and it is
guarded the same way: `scripts/test-a-closed-drm-window-says-so.sh` reads the
series, requires the empty upstream body to be REMOVED, and requires the body
that replaces it to call `delegate_->OnClosed()` -- collected from that hunk
rather than from anywhere in the file, so the call cannot be satisfied by some
other method. No Chromium tree, so it runs in the shell group on every push.

Guidelines audited: docs/guidelines/TESTING.md, docs/guidelines/ERRORS.md,
docs/guidelines/CONTROL_FLOW.md, docs/guidelines/FUNCTIONS.md,
docs/guidelines/FILES.md. Change complies.

Co-Authored-By: Connor Prussin <connor@prussin.net>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0121wHJhHTqCKfhKTwP4R2t1
---
 ui/ozone/platform/drm/host/drm_window_host.cc | 45 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 44 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/ui/ozone/platform/drm/host/drm_window_host.cc b/ui/ozone/platform/drm/host/drm_window_host.cc
index 9ef47dc514..e7ca5f8531 100644
--- a/ui/ozone/platform/drm/host/drm_window_host.cc
+++ b/ui/ozone/platform/drm/host/drm_window_host.cc
@@ -77,7 +77,50 @@ void DrmWindowHost::Hide() {
   visible_ = false;
 }
 
-void DrmWindowHost::Close() {}
+void DrmWindowHost::Close() {
+  // Upstream is an empty body, on the same premise as the NOTREACHED()s below:
+  // ash never routes a window through DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform, so on
+  // ChromeOS nothing ever calls this. A views browser on a tty does, and an
+  // empty body there is a SEGV in the browser process.
+  //
+  // Close() IS A REQUEST, NOT A RELEASE. Its completion is
+  // PlatformWindowDelegate::OnClosed(), and every other Ozone platform says so
+  // by making that call the last thing it does: HeadlessWindow::Close() is
+  // nothing else, WaylandWindow::Close() is nothing else, X11Window::Close()
+  // tears the X window down and then makes it.
+  //
+  // DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform::CloseNow() destroys the compositor and then
+  // calls platform_window()->Close(), and OnClosed() is what nulls the platform
+  // window and finishes the teardown. With no OnClosed() the host is left
+  // half-destroyed -- compositor gone, platform window alive, native widget
+  // alive -- and the next thing to touch it dereferences the destroyed
+  // compositor, which is the crash a 2880x1920 panel produced 200ms after the
+  // modeset it had just confirmed:
+  //
+  //   #4 views::DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform::CloseNow()
+  //   #5 views::DesktopNativeWidgetAura::~DesktopNativeWidgetAura()
+  //   #7 views::Widget::~Widget()
+  //
+  // That frame 4 is the SECOND CloseNow() on the same host. The first ran from
+  // the task Close() posts, passed its `if (!platform_window()) return;` guard,
+  // and ended in DestroyCompositor() plus this no-op. The guard cannot catch
+  // the second, because the platform window this was supposed to close is still
+  // there -- so compositor() is null one line past the guard that was meant to
+  // stop exactly this.
+  //
+  // Upstream states the invariant an empty body breaks, one line into
+  // ~DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform:
+  //
+  //   DCHECK(!platform_window()) << "The host must be closed before destroying it.";
+  //
+  // The shipped engine sets dcheck_always_on = false, so the assertion that
+  // would have named this is compiled out and what is left is the SEGV.
+  //
+  // The delegate destroys this object from inside the call -- OnClosed() ends
+  // in SetPlatformWindow(nullptr) -- so nothing may touch a member afterward.
+  // That is why it is the last statement here, as it is on Wayland and X11.
+  delegate_->OnClosed();
+}
 
 bool DrmWindowHost::IsVisible() const {
   return visible_;
